{
  "Name": "HP/Agilent/Keysight 34401A Digital Multimeter",
  "IdnPattern": "34401A",
  "Terminator": "\n",
  "Notes": "Confirmed against real hardware 2026-09-23 over RS-232: 9600 baud, 8 data bits, 2 stop bits (fixed on this instrument), no parity, no hardware handshake, LF line terminator. Unlike GPIB, RS-232 on the 34401A never auto-enters remote mode - send Remote (SYSTem:REMote) before any query, or the instrument answers with SCPI error +550 \"Command not allowed in local\" and no measurement. Per the programming manual's MEASure?/CONFigure section (chapter 4, page 105/117), the range parameter only accepts a numeric range or MIN|MAX|DEF - DEF selects autoranging. \"AUTO\" is not a valid keyword here (that spelling is only valid on the separate <function>:RANGe:AUTO {OFF|ON} command); the CONFigure command previously offered it as a Range choice, which would be rejected as an illegal parameter by the real instrument and silently no-op - a plausible root cause (not yet re-verified against real hardware) for the previously reported \"Configure DC Voltage Range doesn't appear to do anything\" bug. Fixed here by using \"DEF\" instead of \"AUTO\". Expanded 2026-09-23 to cover the rest of chapter 4's MEASure?/CONFigure/Common/System command set (AC voltage/current, DC current, 2-wire and 4-wire resistance, frequency, period, continuity, diode, plus SYSTem:LOCal/*CLS/*TST?/SYSTem:ERRor?/DISPlay/SYSTem:BEEPer:STATe) - the manual's transcribed reference gives exact numeric range values (0.1/1/10/100/1000) only for the DC voltage function, so every other function's Configure Range choice offers just DEF|MIN|MAX rather than guessing at unconfirmed numeric ranges.",
